A||N||2||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Iron Duke: How to remove a head when there is a broken head bolt||||1||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-24-2001||05:55 AM||||I know it's late, but I just got the head off of a 1984 2.5L 4cylinder.  It is done despite a broken head bolt and three other that could have broken but somehow didn't. Total time: 6 hours<P>How you ask?<P>The engine was removed.  We (had a lot of help from a friend) loosened the head bolts with a medium duty impact wrench.  Four bolts were out in a flash.  Four bolts required a 6 foot "cheater bar" to crack loose.  Then, they would only turn with the 6 foot pipe on a 1/2 drive socket wrench.  They were so tight I broke my 3/8" breaker bar like it was made of cheap plastic.  One of these four turned about five turns and broke.  <P>When we tried to get the head off with gentle prying at the water neck end, it would not budge.  So we got clever and used an engine hoist to "hang" the engine (and stand) about 1" off the ground by it's head.  Though it seeming brutal treatment, the head was unaffected.  I sprayed about a can of WD-40 into all of the head bolt holes and all around the gasket.  Then we lifted hoist again, and no dice.  The head stayed firm.  (INSERT SWEAR WORD HERE)!<P>I took an hour off to go into the house and see my wife.  My buddy went home.  Then, I decided to go out for a smoke.  Once there, I could not resist the challenge of the stuck head.  I hoisted it again and added about 150lb of sandbags to the engine stand.  Then I tried connecting compressed air to the cylinders.  The low pressure leaking sound made me feel more positive as it was clear the head had moved, if only a little.<P>I pulled out my "Silver Slapper" slide hammer kit.  In there was a pointy tool that screwed onto the end of the hammer shaft.  <P>IDEA!<P>I used the slide hammer (with pointy bit) to bang on the broken bolt while the engine supported it's weight and 150lbs more by it's head.  It gave in I tell you!  The head starting coming loose like the Titanic breaking free from the seabed in that 80s classic "Raise the Titanic."<P>Now, the head is in the air above the engine and it's really great to be seeing those pistons all sooty black with 90K miles and 17 years of time on them.  <P>I would have been stuck if I had not read about hanging engines by their heads in this Forum.  Thanks for filling the archives with so many clever ideas!||||reg||1
Z||000001||MrPBody||04-24-2001||07:53 PM||||Glad you got your head un-stuck.  It sounds about as bad as mine: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009150.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009150.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-25-2001||02:28 AM||||MrPBody,<P>I read that thread as it "unfolded."  It was hard to believe when I read it.<P>Now, I know different.  I also know that your thread saved me a lot of time and frustration.  I had that head off in six hours, not a week or more.  I was bummed for maybe two hours, not a week.<P>The struggle was worth it.  I found the oil pan and valve cover to  be free of sludge.  The piston bores look worn, but are free of scratches or blemishes with very even wear.  I'll know more after everything has been magnafluxed, but right now I am starting to believe this engine is in excellent condition.<P>Fieros are funny in a way.  They are really hard to start working on.  But if you hang in there you find a turning point.  Add a touch of sheer denial, and after you're done you'll swear it was all really easy.  Then, when you drive the car you grin and giggle with pride.  <P>Each time you do a big project, you go through the same cycle of difficult and corresponding emotion (at least I always seem to).  But with each of these tasks finished, you get a clearer picture of the way your car will be when it is done and what incredible cars these little GM orphans really are.  <P>No regrets, wallet open and ready . . . ||||reg||6
